From f56a64a589db13ccde76ca21ce0bee35c67203db Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 10:47:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] Host running count: exclude pool-worker-leased cards
 from the file's sync state, not this process's

hermes-hub 2026-11-12 10:35Z (core 4878154a1e95): from a Kanban worker process,
`hermes kanban --board default show t_01a1c7c4e00f5d69 --json` gave queue
{"lane":"local","running":14,"limit":7} ("14/7 running") while the gateway's own
chat_wait_unclaimed notice a minute later said 6/7 and the gateway held 6 running
cards. The other 8 were default-board cards leased to worker-1/3/4/6
(lease_holder != hub, claim_lock NULL).

Cause: kanban_db_connect.connect gives a fenced delegated/worker process a
`mode=ro` connection that skips init, so the file never joins
kanban_db_sync._SYNCED_PATHS; board_mode() then says "off" and
held_elsewhere_sql() excludes nothing. Same for any other-board file opened by
kanban_host_counts._running_in_file in a process that never synced that board.

Fix: kanban_host_counts._running_in reads kanban_sync_state from the file it
counts (mode == 'primary' and that row's gateway_id) and applies the same
held-elsewhere condition as held_elsewhere_sql. No state table, no row, or mode
off/shadow counts every running card; a primary row without gateway_id or an
unreadable state raises, so running_on_host reports None (unknown).
board_mode()/held_elsewhere_sql() and the dispatcher's write paths are
unchanged; nothing is opened writable.

Proof: tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_host_counts_held_elsewhere.py (10 tests):
4 RED on base (worker ro read of a 5 own + 8 leased primary board, other-board
file read, unreadable state, primary without gateway_id), all GREEN after.
6 hand mutants of the new conditions all killed. 15 test files importing
kanban_queue_position/host_counts/parent_unclaimed/chat_wait: 162 passed.
Removed test_card_running_under_another_gateways_lease_is_not_a_local_slot,
which pinned the old seam by monkeypatching held_elsewhere_sql; the new file
covers it with real primary-board rows.

Card: t_01a1cf5749db0a43
